--- body ---
## Context

A PR body's `**Ticket:** tickets/NNNN-...` line does not close anything by
itself. It is executed by `erg-pr-merge`, which runs `erg close` and then
`erg archive` on the named ticket. A bare `gh pr merge` bypasses that entirely,
and the claim is dropped with no output.

Nothing downstream notices. `erg check` passes either way — an open ticket is
not an error — so a dropped close and a clean merge produce identical evidence.

**Observed 2026-09-10, git-erg PR #334**: the fix for ticket 0276 landed on
`main` while 0276 stayed in `tickets/` with no `Closed:` header. It took a
`/perch` pass to notice, and a second PR (#335) to repair.

This ticket closes the gap on both sides: prevention at the moment of merge,
and detection for the merge routes a hook cannot see.

## Why the existing machinery does not cover it

**The hook that already intercepts `gh pr merge` steers toward the defect.**
`scripts/block-pr-merge-in-worktree.sh` fires on this exact command, and its
block message recommends:

    gh api "repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/$PR/merge" -X PUT -f merge_method=squash

which is the very bypass above. Its stated premise — that `gh pr merge` aborts
in every linked worktree — was refuted on 2026-09-08: `erg-pr-merge` calls it
from worktrees, and did so for PRs 808 through 826 without a failure.

The refutation does not reach the whole guard. `gh pr merge --merge` is a pure
API call touching no local git, but `--delete-branch` does check out and delete
locally, and can genuinely fail against the parent checkout's ref lock. So the
guard was right about one flag and over-broad about the command from birth.
Narrow it; do not delete the true part with the false part.

**The detector that exists covers the other half of the failure.**
`/healthcheck` step 9 reports `tickets.closed_unarchived` — tickets carrying a
`Closed:` header that were never moved to `tickets/closed/`. That is the
close-without-*archive* escape. On PR #334 there was no `Closed:` header at
all: `erg close` never ran, so there was nothing to archive. Step 9 indexes on
the presence of `Closed:`, which makes it blind by construction to a ticket
that was never closed — indistinguishable, to it, from a ticket still legitimately
open.

The missing check is the other side of the join: *merged PRs carrying a close
claim → is that ticket actually closed?*

## Relevant files

- `scripts/block-pr-merge-in-worktree.sh` — the hook to narrow and repurpose;
  its name asserts the refuted premise, so it is renamed
- `tests/test_block_pr_merge_in_worktree.sh` — its test, renamed with it
- `settings.shared.json`, `adapters/claude-code/hooks/hooks.json` — the wiring
  that names the script path (`scripts/check-settings-drift.sh` compares them)
- `skills/roar/SKILL.md` — step 7 (`**Close** the ticket if still open`) is the
  natural home for the detector: it is already the ticket-closure step
- `skills/roar/enumerate-merges.py` — reference for the close-commit template
  the detector must recognise

## Actions

1. Rename the guard to `scripts/guard-gh-pr-merge.sh`, joining the existing
   `guard-*.sh` family, and update both wiring files.
2. Narrow the worktree block to `--delete-branch` / `-d` only — the one flag
   the 2026-09-08 measurement did not refute.
3. Add the close-claim warning, gated on a `tickets/` directory being present
   in the repo the command runs in. Warn (exit 0 + stdout), do not block: the
   author asked for an advisory, and a repo may have legitimate bare merges.
   No network call — a `PreToolUse` hook runs under a 5 s timeout, and a
   timed-out body fetch yields an all-clear indistinguishable from
   "I could not look".
4. Add `skills/roar/check-close-claims.sh`: for each PR merged since a given
   point, read its body, extract a close claim, and report any whose ticket has
   no `Closed:` header. This covers the routes the hook cannot see — the forge
   web UI, another machine, another session.
5. Wire it into `/roar` step 7 without renumbering: `git.md` cites "roar step
   10" and `workflow.md` cites "/roar step 8", and step 2's snippet is executed
   verbatim by `tests/test_roar_step2_attribution.py`, which must keep seeing
   exactly one runnable block.

## Test

Red first, on the guard: a payload carrying `gh pr merge 449 --merge` in a repo
with a `tickets/` directory must produce the close-claim warning. Against the
current script it produces a worktree block instead.

**The positive control is mandatory on the detector**, because its natural
output is silence. Build a fixture with a merged PR whose body carries
`**Ticket:** tickets/0001-x.erg` and a `tickets/0001-x.erg` with no `Closed:`
header, and assert the detector names it. A fixture where every claim was
honoured cannot tell a working detector from one that never looked.

## Verification

- [ ] `bash tests/test_guard_gh_pr_merge.sh` passes, including the warning case
- [ ] The new test run against the OLD script fails on exactly the new
      assertions — a test passing against both proves nothing about the change
- [ ] The detector names a known-bad fixture, and stays quiet on one where every
      claim was honoured
- [ ] The detector's live run reports counts that add up to what it examined
- [ ] `tests/test_roar_step2_attribution.py` still passes (step 2 untouched)
- [ ] `make check` passes

**Deployment note, found while doing this.** The rename needs the live
`~/.claude/settings.json` re-aligned by hand: it names the old script path, and
`scripts/check-settings-drift.sh` is advisory (exit 0) and already reports
pre-existing drift on `hooks`, so it will not stop a machine from running a hook
that points at a deleted file. One `sed` per machine after this merges:

    sed -i 's#block-pr-merge-in-worktree.sh#guard-gh-pr-merge.sh#' ~/.claude/settings.json

## Invariants

- `erg-pr-merge` remains the sanctioned path and never trips the hook: it is a
  script, so the matcher never sees the string `gh pr merge` coming from it.
- No renumbering of `/roar` steps — resident rules cite step 8 and step 10.
- `--delete-branch` in a linked worktree stays blocked.
- `make check` passes.

## Exit criteria

- A bare `gh pr merge` in a repo with a ticket store produces a visible warning
  naming `/merge`, at the moment of merge rather than days later.
- A merged PR whose close claim was dropped is named by `/roar`, whatever route
  merged it.
- The guard's name and header describe what it now does, with the refuted
  premise removed and the surviving narrow case documented.
